  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Read this as the target design",
      "items": [
        "Grounded in the 15 October architecture and epic #8950; this is not a live deployment status map.",
        "Four factories are ownership and lifecycle boundaries, not a requirement for four microservices.",
        "Management services coordinate resources; training controllers and jobs run on workspace clusters."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Recovery and truthful readiness",
      "items": [
        "Each factory waits for fresh Ready evidence for the current desired revision; applying a declaration is not completion.",
        "Argo reconciles declarations. Factory controllers observe cloud resources and use governed execution for approved changes.",
        "Superplane submits each TrainJob separately. Argo does not recreate finished runs. Retain attempts, fences and resource identities."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Cut over the existing workspace",
      "items": [
        "Repair and verify the current workspace, then shadow-adopt its resource IDs and Terraform backend, lineage and history.",
        "Fence the old writer before activating one new owner; evaluate fallback against the retained state.",
        "R04 uses already qualified R1 artifacts. Preserve the cluster and namespace; no infrastructure recreation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Three grouped Superplane releases",
      "items": [
        "R1: AWS factories, central Argo, experience and migration. R2: training, capacity and results. R3: Azure, GCP and Nebius.",
        "Epic #8950 has 57 implementation stories and 11 release, verification and cutover stories in eight workstreams.",
        "Gateway keeps rolling independently; qualify the actual serving API, scheduler and binding for each release. MLflow placement remains optional and undecided."
      ]
    }
  ]
